.TH DF 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
df \- report free disk space
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B df
.RB [ \-k ]
.RI [ file .\|.\|.]
.SH DESCRIPTION
For each
.I file
operand,
.B df
writes one line describing the amount of space available on the
filesystem containing
.IR file .
.I file
may name a file, a directory, or anything else that resolves to a
location on a filesystem; the filesystem
.I file
resides on is what is reported, not
.IR file 's
own size.
.PP
If no
.I file
operand is given,
.B df
reports on the filesystem containing the current working directory
only, as if
.B .
had been given as the sole operand. There is no support for
enumerating or reporting on every filesystem mounted on the system; a
filesystem is reported only when named (directly, or via its
containing directory) by an operand, or implied by the current
directory when no operand is given.
.PP
The report has six columns:
.BR Filesystem ,
the block count column (labeled
.B 512\-blocks
or
.BR 1024\-blks ,
depending on
.BR \-k ),
.BR Used ,
.BR Available ,
.BR Capacity ,
and
.BR "Mounted on" .
Because this platform has no device-pathname namespace and no separate
notion of "the device a filesystem lives on" versus "where it is
mounted", both the
.B Filesystem
and
.B "Mounted on"
columns show the same thing: the (resolved)
.I file
operand itself \(em an honest report of what was queried, not an
invented device name.
.PP
.B Used
is the total block count minus the free block count (not minus the
available block count): space that an unprivileged process cannot use
is still not counted as
.IR used .
.B Capacity
is
.RI ( Used / ( Used + Available )),
rounded up to the next whole percent; because
.B Used
and
.B Available
do not always sum to the filesystem's total block count (space
reserved for a privileged user counts toward neither
.B Available
nor the difference that produces
.BR Used ),
.B Capacity
can exceed 100%.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-k
Report the block count, used, and available columns in units of
1024\-byte blocks instead of the default 512\-byte blocks.
.PP
A
.B \-\-
argument ends option parsing, so that a
.I file
operand beginning with
.B \-
can still be given unambiguously.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I file
The pathname of a file or directory whose containing filesystem should
be reported on.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every operand (or, with no operands, the current directory's own
filesystem) was queried successfully.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option was given, or querying the filesystem for at least
one operand failed (e.g., the operand named a nonexistent path);
processing continues across the remaining operands regardless.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR du (1)
